My experimental skills


Q1. A wet lab technique I have done: PCR + gel electrophoresis

Flow path (what I did):
I collected the sample (blood or tissue).

I extracted DNA (lysis → protein removal → DNA precipitation → wash).

I checked DNA quality on NanoDrop (concentration and A260/280 purity).

I set up the PCR master mix (buffer, dNTPs, primers, Taq polymerase, template, water).

I ran thermal cycling (denaturation → annealing → extension, 30-35 cycles).

I ran gel electrophoresis (agarose gel + DNA ladder + loading dye).

I visualized the gel under UV or a gel doc system.

I interpreted the result (band size vs ladder, band present or absent).

Purpose and practical use:
I use PCR to make millions of copies of one DNA region so I can study it.
I use it for gene detection, mutation or variant checking, pathogen detection, and sample preparation for sequencing.

I use the gel to confirm that my product is the right size and that the reaction worked.

How I say it: "I extracted DNA, checked its purity, amplified my target region by PCR, and confirmed the product on an agarose gel. This confirmation step is important before sequencing or any downstream analysis."

An experiment that failed
Situation: My PCR gave no band, or multiple non-specific bands, on the gel.
How I troubleshot it:
I checked my controls. The positive control worked, so my reagents were fine. The negative control was clean, so there was no contamination.

I checked my template DNA. The A260/280 ratio was low, which suggested protein or phenol contamination.

I checked my primers. My annealing temperature was too low, which caused non-specific binding.

How I fixed it:
I re-purified the DNA.
I ran a gradient PCR to find the best annealing temperature.
I made fresh primer dilutions.
I used a hot-start polymerase.
I repeated the PCR and got a single sharp band of the correct size.

What I learned: I always run positive and negative controls, and I check DNA quality before PCR. I change one variable at a time, so I know exactly what the problem was.
Why this answer matters: It shows that I can troubleshoot logically, not just follow a protocol.


Quick tips for my works: 
I do in this order: what I did → why → what went wrong → how I fixed it → what I learned.

I name one number or detail (annealing temperature, A260/280 value, number of cycles). It makes my answer sound real.

My strength is genomics and data, so I add: "I connect this wet lab data with my bioinformatics engineering and analysis."



qPCR (real-time PCR)

Flow path (what I did):
I collected the sample (blood, tissue, or cells).

I extracted RNA (for gene expression) or DNA.

I checked quality on NanoDrop (concentration, A260/280).

I made cDNA by reverse transcription (for RNA samples).

I designed and validated my primers (efficiency 90-110%).

I set up the reaction: SYBR Green or TaqMan master mix, primers, cDNA, water. 

I ran everything in triplicate with a no-template control (NTC) and a reference gene (e.g., GAPDH or β-actin).

I ran it on the real-time machine (40 cycles) with a melt curve at the end.

I analyzed the data: Ct values → ΔCt → ΔΔCt → fold change.

I did statistics and made a graph (e.g., t-test, bar chart).

Purpose and practical uses:
I use qPCR to measure how much of a gene or DNA is present, not just yes or no.

Gene expression: I check if a gene is up or down in disease vs normal.

Validation: I confirm RNA-seq or microarray results.

Pathogen detection and viral load (e.g., desired cell testing).

Copy number and mutation or genotyping assays.

Biomarker testing for diagnostics.

How I say it: "I used qPCR to measure gene expression. I made cDNA, ran my samples in triplicate with a reference gene, checked the melt curve for specificity, and calculated fold change with the ΔΔCt method.”


Problems faced , cause and how I solved them? 

Prob: Amplification in NTC (negative control)
Cause: Contamination or primer-dimers
Solution: Used fresh aliquots, separate pre- and post-PCR areas, filter tips, cleaned the bench with 10% bleach

Prob: Multiple peaks in melt curve

Cause: Non-specific products or primer-dimers

Sol: Redesigned primers, raised annealing temperature, lowered primer concentration

Prob: High Ct variation between triplicates
Cause: Pipetting error
Sol: Made a master mix, used calibrated pipettes, reverse pipetting


Prob: Late Ct (above 35) or no signal

Cause: Poor RNA quality or low template

Sol: Re-extracted RNA, checked RIN or A260/280, added more cDNA


Prob: Poor primer efficiency
Cause: Bad primer design
Sol: Ran a standard curve (serial dilution), checked slope, redesigned primers


Prob: Unstable reference gene
Cause: Reference gene changed between conditions

Sol: Tested several reference genes and picked the stable one



Best story to tell (pick one):

"My melt curve showed two peaks, so my primers were amplifying more than one product. I checked primer-dimers on a gel, redesigned the primers, ran a temperature gradient, and got a single sharp peak. After that, I checked efficiency with a standard curve before running my real samples."

What I learned try to always validate primers first, always run the NTC and a reference gene, and never trust Ct values without checking the melt curve.


Know the difference from PCR: 

PCR gives an end-point result (a band on a gel). 

qPCR gives quantitative, real-time data.
Link to my strength: "I validated my RNA-seq differentially expressed genes with qPCR."


